    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d663">

                <pb facs="0889" n="877" sameAs="frus:frus1946v08/pg877"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.11B/5–1046</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of Conversation, by the Director of the
                        Office of Far Eastern Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Vincent</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-05-10">May 10, 1946</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <table>
                    <row role="label">
                        <cell rendition="#top #nowrap">Participants:</cell>
                        <cell>His Excellency Mr. Roxas, President-elect of the Philippine
                            Commonwealth;</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell>Mr. McNutt, High Commissioner to the Philippines,</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell>Mr. Acheson,</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell>Mr. Vincent</cell>
                    </row>
                </table>
                <p>The President-elect called at 3:15, accompanied by Mr. McNutt.<anchor n="28"
                        xml:id="d663-f1"/></p>
                <pb facs="0890" n="878" xml:id="pg878"/>
                <p>During the course of the conversation Mr. Roxas mentioned the various treaties
                    that are now being prepared in the State Department for the establishment of
                    relations between the United States and the Philippines. He said that he would
                    be glad to look these treaties over while he was here and to take drafts back
                    with him to Manila. He expressed confidence that the treaties as we drafted them
                    would be acceptable to the Philippine Government but that there might be minor
                    suggestions which he could assure us we would have within a week after his
                    return to Manila.<anchor n="29" xml:id="d663-f2"/></p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d663-f1" n="28" xml:id="d663-f1-content">
                <p>For statement by President Truman on the visit of president-elect Roxas and their
                    meeting on May 10, see Department of State <hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Bulletin</hi>, May 19, 1946, p.867.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d663-f2" n="29" xml:id="d663-f2-content">
                <p>In response to Mr. Roxas’ request that the United States represent Philippine
                    interests abroad after July 4 until the Philippines was ready, Mr. Acheson
                    replied affirmatively and suggested that a formal approach on this subject be
                    made after Mr. Roxas assumed office. (704.11B00/5–1046) On May 15 Mr. Roxas
                    indicated he would not have time to discuss proposed general commercial treaty
                    and Executive trade agreement drafts due to preoccupation with the proposed
                    military base treaty and other matters (611.11B31/5–1546); texts were
                    subsequently sent to Manila for presentation. (711.11B2/6–2546;
                    611.11B31/6–1746, 6–2146; 711.11B/6–2446, 6–1346).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
